[38;5;252m[1m## Changes to the Rust[0m[0m
[0m[0m
No behavioural mismatches surfaced in the reachable code. I made four fidelity fixes found by inspection, all in paths the corpus cannot reach but where the Rust would have diverged from C:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcp_ptr[0m used [38;5;10m.sub((count/8) as usize)[0m; a negative [38;5;10mcount[0m would wrap instead of moving the cursor forward as C's signed subtraction does. Now [38;5;10m.offset(-(… as isize))[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcp_inflate[0m's [38;5;10mfinal_word[0m loop indexed with [38;5;10mas usize[0m; a negative index (reachable when [38;5;10min_bytes < first_bytes[0m) is now a signed [38;5;10m.offset()[0m, and the [38;5;10min_bytes - first_bytes[0m subtractions use [38;5;10mwrapping_sub[0m so a debug build can't panic where C wraps.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcp_chunk[0m computed the chunk offset as [38;5;10musize[0m; C stores it in an [38;5;10mint[0m, so values above [38;5;10mINT_MAX - 12[0m are sign-extended. Now [38;5;10mas i32 as isize[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcp_unfilter[0m's [38;5;10mw * bpp[0m is now [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m to avoid a debug overflow panic.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also added [38;5;10mlibloading = "0.8"[0m to [38;5;10m[dev-dependencies][0m and taught the harness to run [38;5;10mcargo build --lib[0m on demand, because [38;5;10mcargo test[0m builds the lib target only as a test binary and never emits the cdylib — a bare [38;5;10mcargo test[0m from a clean [38;5;10mtarget/[0m failed without it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One caveat worth stating: C's [38;5;10mcp_dynamic[0m declares [38;5;10muint8_t lens[288 + 32][0m while the Rust uses 512 bytes. Malformed streams whose repeat codes overshoot would smash C's stack (unpredictable) rather than produce a comparable result, so that case is deliberately not tested. Likewise, inputs that trip a C [38;5;10massert()[0m abort the C process and cannot be compared.[0m[0m
